let's get some reaction to what we've  just heard from  labour's shadow health secretary  jonathan  ashworth who joins us now  um mr ashworth uh  do you think that rob generic has a case  that  the chancellor and the prime minister  are they've been pinged and they  basically just have to do what they're  told  um and it's just unfortunate  i think there'll be parents across the  country  who have struggled this past year when  their  children have been sent home because  they were in a bubble and had to isolate  there will be workers across the country  who have to isolate when pinged  including in public services  like the nhs and i think for many of  them  waking up this morning to hear that  there is a  special rule an exclusive rule for boris  johnson  and rishi sunak they will be saying to  themselves  that this looks like one rule for them  and something else  for the rest of us nobody understands  how you can get  access to this special special treatment  this special vip lane  where you don't have to isolate yourself  if it is a pilot  why can't employers apply for the  workforce to be  members of this pilot why can't schools  have applied to be part of this pilot  test  i really do think a lot of people are  going to be looking at this and thinking  what on earth is going on and of course  this is a time when we need to maintain  confidence in the isolation policies  because isolation taking yourself away  from society  if you've been in contact with someone  who's got the virus is one of the key  ways in which we break transmission  and of course we know that infections  are rising at the moment  let's be uh fair here  if it were sakir stama in number 10  and you were the health secretary  you would be coming on here and saying  look the man is the prime minister  of course we have to take special steps  because uh  he runs the country and we can't have  the prime minister  uh inhibited from doing his job  i'm not sure how the prime minister  joining zoom calls from his office  in the flat above downing street  inhibits him doing his job to be frank  he's still able to use the telephone  he's still able to use  a computer and he's still able to join a  zoom call as  lots of us have had to do in these past  16 months  throughout the crisis i think why it  sticks in people's throat is that  apparently there is this special system  this vip system that you  that somehow some people can get on get  onto where they can avoid  isolating if they've been pinged now if  there is this system  then other people should be able to  apply for it other employers should be  able to apply for it and ask for their  workforce to be considered that's not  the case at the moment so for  so it looks like for a lot of us it is  indeed one rule for  them at the top and another for the rest  of us  okay well let's just talk about the  actual policy rather than the politics  of  this morning um there are a number of  things which are in the air  for example the proposal for test and  release  a version of it supported by your former  leader tony blair this morning i.e  that people who can can be tested every  day  and if they're negative they can get on  with their  jobs would you support that  being uh applied across the population  well sage are looking at it and i think  we should wait and see  for the sage final analysis of it of  course the problem with people getting  pings the problem with people being  asked to isolate is that is a symptom  of increased transmission in society now  we know that we've had over 50 000  cases we know that the virus is climbing  again  very steeply so what you've got to do is  put in  measures put measures in place to break  transmission chains but instead of doing  that we're about to have freedom day  tomorrow where the government where  sajid javid and others are effectively  putting their foot down on the  accelerator  and throwing off the seat belts because  precautionary measures  like mask wearing like encouraging  people to work from home like paying  people decent sick pay  are not being implemented i think that  is a mistake  this clear so when you and other labor  spokespeople have been saying  for the last two or three weeks you  don't oppose freedom day  what you really meant was that you do  oppose opening up on tomorrow  no we want to proceed with caution and i  would have an approach which  is about protecting what's due tomorrow  then  just to be clear what would you not do  tomorrow  well i would not get rid of the monetary  mass wearing look  people want people will want to uh do  the right thing and they'll want to wear  masks  but they want to be reassured that in  return that they are protected by others  wearing masks  but getting rid of the mandatory rule i  think is too much of a risk  tomorrow we're likely to see possibly  the busiest day on the transport  networks  for 16 months for people going back to  work i would say allow people to  continue to work from home  if they want to i would say let's let's  mobilize british manufacturing  to build air filtration units to put in  our schools and our premises  i would pay people sick pay and i will  prepare for the future  by investing in the national health  service now and because  we know  is pretty much what conservative  ministers come on here and say  so other than moving from mandatory  mask wearing to voluntary mask wearing  which  surveys in the last few days show us  that i think three quarters maybe more  of people will do anyway that's the  difference between  you and the government no and with  respect  i don't think it i haven't seen  conservative ministers coming on the  newspaper let me assure you you  obviously haven't watched a program but  that's what they've been saying pretty  much what you've just said  they don't talk about sick pay they  don't say they're going to fix sick pay  they don't say they're going to give  people the right to work from home  should they want t